# DESCRIPTION

hda drives an HD Audio controller and the codec on its link (Intel High Definition Audio Specification, rev. 1.0a), and serves them as a sound device, audio(3).
devmgr(8) starts one for each PCI function of the audio device class, as QEMU's ich9-intel-hda is, and binds its directory at `/dev/audio/`<n>:

```text
driver hda
class audio
match pci-class 04.03
```

The driver resets the controller and takes as its codecs those that `STATESTS` names before the reset or after it, or, if neither names one, those of addresses 0 to 7 that answer.
Commands go through the CORB, 256 entries, and their responses are read by polling the RIRB, whose interrupt stays off; after each response the RIRB's status flags are cleared, so a controller that counts responses keeps taking commands.
Registers are accessed at the widths the specification gives them; the controller's `SDnCTL` is three bytes and `SDnSTS` the fourth.

From the first codec with an audio function group it reads every widget: its type, its connections (either form of the list, ranges included), a pin's capabilities and configuration default, amplifiers and PCM formats.
Playback's path runs from the first connected output pin, line out before speaker before headphones by their defaults, through mixers and selectors to an analog DAC, at most 6 nodes; capture's from an analog ADC to the first connected input pin, line in before microphone.
Each path is set up once: every node powered, each selector set to the path, each amplifier on it unmuted at 0 dB, the pin's output (with its headphone amplifier for headphones, and EAPD where it has it) or input enabled.
The streams take what the DAC's or ADC's PCM capabilities give: 16, 24 (`s24`) or 32-bit samples at the rates it lists, one channel or two.

Each direction uses the controller's first stream descriptor of its kind, output stream tag 1 and input tag 2.
Its cyclic buffer, up to 256 KiB, is memory the controller reaches through its own DMA, described by a BDL of one entry a period, each raising its interrupt when done; periods must be multiples of 128 bytes.
The descriptor runs at the first period handed to it and reads or writes the buffer by itself after that.
At each interrupt the driver reads each running stream's position (`SDnLPIB`) and counts every period it has moved past, so an interrupt that came late, or covered two periods, loses none.
Stopping a stream clears `RUN` and resets the descriptor, holding `SRST` 100 µs.
The interrupt is INTx with a 50 ms poll against a lost one; a controller's MSI isn't used.

# EXAMPLES

QEMU's controller and codec, as boot-audio-hda adds them:

```text
-audiodev none,id=snd0
-device ich9-intel-hda -device hda-duplex,audiodev=snd0
```

# FILES

The driver's tree is served in devmgr(8)'s, at `drivers/hda-<address>`.

: `audio`
  The device's audio(3) directory, which devmgr binds at `/dev/audio/`<n>.
: `status`
  Read only.
  `interrupt` (`MSI-X` or `INTx`); `streams in <n> out <n>` (the controller's descriptors); `codecs` (the mask found), the codec used, its `vendor` and `widgets`; `output path` and `input path`, their nodes from the pin's end or `none`; `periods out <n> in <n>`; `commands`, `refused` (path verbs the codec didn't answer) and `unsolicited`; `interrupts <n> polls <n>`.
  If the controller or codec didn't come up, a line saying why and `gcap`, `gctl` and `statests` as they read, then `FAILED: status <n>`.

